<https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme/concept418> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme/concept417> ;
    skos:definition "Conventional three-band photography from a spaceborne platform. Its operating principle is based on the detection of reflected electromagnetic radiation between 400 nm and 700 nm (i.e. light), sometimes extended with one near-infrared band (between 700 nm and 1100 nm)."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme> ;
    skos:prefLabel "weltraumgestützte Fotografie"@de,
        "spaceborne photography"@en .

<https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme/concept419> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme/concept417> ;
    skos:definition "Imaging reflected (or sometimes emitted) optical electromagnetic radiation with wavelengths that usually fall between 315 nm and 3000 nm from a space-borne platform. In contrast to multi-spectral imaging, imaging spectroscopy or hyper-spectral imaging captures the incoming radiation in more than ten up to hundreds of small, contiguous spectral bands that often have a bandwidth of about 10 nm."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme> ;
    skos:prefLabel "weltraumgestützte Abbildungsspektroskopie"@de,
        "spaceborne imaging spectroscopy"@en .

<https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme/concept420> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme/concept417> ;
    skos:definition "Imaging reflected (or sometimes emitted) optical electromagnetic radiation with wavelengths that usually fall between 315 nm and 3000 nm from a space-borne platform. In contrast to imaging spectroscopy and standard photography, multi-spectral imaging captures the incoming radiation in four up to ten adjacent spectral bands that are commonly between 20 nm to 100 nm wide."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme> ;
    skos:prefLabel "weltraumgestützte multispektrale Bildgebung"@de,
        "spaceborne multispectral imaging"@en .

<https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme/concept421> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme/concept417> ;
    skos:definition "Scanning of Earth's surface using laser profilers mounted in satellites."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme> ;
    skos:prefLabel "weltraumgestütztes Laser-Scanning"@de,
        "spaceborne laser scanning"@en .

<https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme/concept422> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme/concept417> ;
    skos:definition "Imaging a portion of a scene’s self-emitted thermal radiant energy from a space-borne platform in one or more spectral bands. Thermal imaging usually uses thermal electromagnetic radiation in the long wavelength infrared (LWIR; 6 μm to 15 μm) and far/extreme-infrared (FIR; 15 μm to 1 mm) regions. As a result, the MWIR to FIR optical region is commonly denoted the thermal region."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme> ;
    skos:prefLabel "weltraumgestützter Thermografie"@de,
        "spaceborne thermography"@en .

<https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme/concept423> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme/concept417> ;
    skos:definition "Scanning of Earth's surface using radar (microwave) profilers mounted in satellites from the altitude of ca. 250-800 km. Since radar wavelenghts are much longer than those of visible or infra-red light, an imaging radar can \"see\" through cloudy or dusty conditions."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme> ;
    skos:prefLabel "weltraumgestütztes Radarbild"@de,
        "spaceborne radar imaging"@en .

<https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme/concept426> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme/concept425> ;
    skos:definition "Conventional three-band photography from an airborne platform. Its operating principle is based on the detection of reflected electromagnetic radiation between 400 nm and 700 nm (i.e. light), sometimes extended with one near-infrared band (between 700 nm and 1100 nm). The total coverage approach aims at photographing a complete area and is usually used for (non-archaeological) mapping projects. It therefore contrast with the observer-directed approach which is prevalent in archaeology. This approach can yield both vertical and as well oblique images."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Gesamtabdeckung"@de,
        "total covarage"@en .

<https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme/concept427> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme/concept425> ;
    skos:definition "Conventional three-band photography from an airborne platform. Its operating principle is based on the detection of reflected electromagnetic radiation between 400 nm and 700 nm (i.e. light), sometimes extended with one near-infrared band (between 700 nm and 1100 nm). In archaeology, the observer-directed approach is often used from the cabin of a low-flying (anywhere between 300 m and higher) aircraft. After an archaeological or environmental feature of interest is detected, it is orbited and photographed from various positions. Since this type of archaeological aerial prospection primarily generates oblique images (or simply ‘obliques’) of archaeological interest, archaeologists have used ‘oblique survey’ as shorthand for this type of observer-directed or observer-biased survey strategy."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme> ;
    skos:prefLabel "beobachtungsbezogene Luftbildfotografie"@de,
        "observer directed"@en .

<https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme/concept428> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme/concept424> ;
    skos:definition "Imaging a portion of a scene’s self-emitted thermal radiant energy from an airborne platform in one or more spectral bands. Thermal imaging usually uses thermal electromagnetic radiation in the long wavelength infrared (LWIR; 6 μm to 15 μm) and far/extreme-infrared (FIR; 15 μm to 1 mm) regions. As a result, the MWIR to FIR optical region is commonly denoted the thermal region."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://vocabs.acdh.oeaw.ac.at/iadthesaurus/scheme> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Luftthermografie"@de,
        "aerial thermography"@en .

